t from a general consumer or harness failure and measure the bounded client endpoint. The main reply's log index is a later committed index, so no stale-read value or safety violation is established. The request remains uncompleted after a valid applied index until the measured logical timeout.\",\n      \"knowledge\": [\n        {\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"827f420be72d403f9a804a543fac77cc-knowledge-0\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"The confirming context returns earlier queued requests while preserving each request's own SystemCtx.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"827f420be72d403f9a804a543fac77cc-knowledge-1\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"Remote replies are tagged with the confirming heartbeat context instead of the queued request's context.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"827f420be72d403f9a804a543fac77cc-knowledge-2\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"A response with a nonmatching SystemCtx leaves the original pending batch at index zero and cannot complete it.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"083048b436e74eaa9a8ec611b31527e4-knowledge-0\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"The exercised later-confirmation ordering produced a mismatched reply context and left the earlier read batch unready after the ready index was applied.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"1457862374ac4b5bbba3c0e3588dbb50-knowledge-0\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"The sequential-confirmation control completed when the admitted context was confirmed first.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"entry_id\": \"@wrong-context-read-times-out\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"The logical-deadline case returned RequestTimeout at tick 51 and removed the stale batch.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"counterevidence\": [\n        \"The source path that constructs the remote reply uses m.Hint and m.HintHigh; no re-tagging occurs between that send and node 2's consumer in the main probe.\",\n        \"The main reply's log index was 4 and the ready index was 4, so neither the main nor timeout execution shows a stale committed index or stale application state.\",\n        \"The late context-101 confirmation produced zero context-correct replies and completion remained zero.\",\n        \"The sequential control completed with the admitted context, so the main failure is not a general inability of the harness or pending-read consumer to complete.\",\n        \"The timeout receipt shows a client timeout and cleanup, not a successful stale read; it measures the bounded endpoint without establishing a safety violation.\"\n      ],\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"The ILogDB is test-only and in-memory; disk failure, restart, snapshots, and real transport delay were not exercised.\",\n        \"The main test applies the returned ready index synchronously; the companion timeout receipt measures the logical deadline at tick 51, not wall-clock scheduling.\",\n        \"The exercised scope is a three-voter in-process group with follower-originated reads. Other membership layouts and observer-originated reads were not measured.\",\n        \"The sequential control holds only for its exercised first-context ordering; it does not establish safety or completion for all schedules.\",\n        \"The observed wrong reply context is a correlation defect; it is not by itself evidence that the returned read index is stale or that a wrong data value was read.\"\n      ],\n      \"joint_history\": \"The main, control, and timeout receipts are separate fixed experiments from the same harness family and three-peer in-memory setup. The main experiment withholds node 2's earlier heartbeat and confirms node 3's later context first. The control confirms node 2's admitted context first on the same handlers and pending-read consumer. The timeout experiment repeats the later-first ordering and advances the pending-read logical clock past the caller's 50-tick deadline. The differences are intentional ordering and deadline comparisons; the timeout case does not claim a result under a correct-context reply.\",\n      \"failure_attribution\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
